      ],
      "explanation": "Because \\(3^{2x}=(3^x)^2\\), the equation has a quadratic pattern in the repeated quantity \\(3^x\\). Moving everything to one side and factoring gives\n\\[\n(3^x)^2-10(3^x)+9=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n(3^x-1)(3^x-9)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so \\(3^x=1=3^0\\) or \\(3^x=9=3^2\\). Since \\(3^x\\) is strictly increasing for real \\(x\\), these give exactly \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=2\\). Both satisfy the original equation.\n\nConsequently,\n\\[\nx^2+1=0^2+1=1\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nx^2+1=2^2+1=5.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(1\\) or \\(5\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
      "id": "factor-exponential-expression",
